diagnosis_text:
The helpdesk report claims that `web1.local` has been timing out from `host_1_1_1_1` all morning. A full four-phase diagnostic sweep finds no fault anywhere in the path.

**Affected component:** None confirmed active.

**Mechanism (checked):** (1) L2/L3 identity — `infra_sweep` and `l2_snapshot` confirmed zero ACL rules, no duplicate MACs, no static ARP poison, and correct IP/gateway/resolver configuration on every device. (2) Routing fabric — `ospf_snapshot` confirmed all six FRR routers healthy, every OSPF neighbor in Full state, 10 routes each; no missing prefixes or adjacency gaps exist on the path host_1_1_1_1 → switch_dist_1_1 → router_core_1 → switch_server_access → web_server_1. (3) Service layer — `service_snapshot` confirmed that `web1.local` resolves correctly to `10.200.0.4` from `host_1_1_1_1`, and an HTTP GET to `http://web1.local/` returns HTTP 200 in approximately 5 ms right now. `web_server_1` also responds to its own localhost probe with HTTP 200 instantly. (4) Resource pressure — `pressure_sweep` found no stress tools, no CPU spikes, no socket floods, and no daemon anomalies on any of the 15 devices.

**Localization:** None — all devices and paths are healthy.

**Root cause:** The reported timeout is a past or transient event that has since resolved on its own; no sustaining mechanism, no fault trace, and no ACL, link, routing, or service anomaly remain in evidence.

**Fix:** No corrective action is required at this time. If the issue recurs, capture `curl -v http://web1.local/` output from `host_1_1_1_1` and check `web_server_1` HTTP access logs at that moment to pinpoint whether the failure originates at DNS, TCP connect, or the HTTP layer.
=== END ===